# mm.stdio

A portable console. It moves bytes to and from wherever the selected platform's
console goes, which on one board is a UART, on another a USB CDC endpoint, and
under a debugger a semihosting call.

This is a different question from mm.mcu's UART facility. uart_write names a
peripheral; this names a role, and the platform decides what carries it. A board
with both binds one here and reaches the other through mm.mcu.

write reports how much it accepted. That is the whole design rather than a
detail: a console whose far end is absent or not reading cannot deliver
everything, and an interface that returned only a status would have to either
block until it could, or claim a delivery it did not make. Neither is an honest
answer for a transport that may have nobody on the other end.

read with nothing available is Ok with a count of zero, as no contact is in
mm.touch. Nothing waiting is an answer.

connected is a query rather than a wait. A caller that wants to wait polls it
and bounds the loop itself; putting a timeout here would push one policy onto
every platform. A console that cannot tell whether anything is listening answers
Unsupported rather than guessing.

There is no formatting. No printf, no iostreams, no <cstdio>: formatting picks a
locale, a float representation, and a buffer, and none of those belong in a
transport. A caller that wants text writes the bytes of the text it composed.
There is no line discipline and no baud, parity, or flow control either; the
first is policy and the others are meaningless on a packet transport.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected platform's console provider part
of an executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.stdio.
